  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 735 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The Tornado Watch has been cancelled. The threat for\nsevere weather is diminishing this evening, though locally \nheavy rain cannot be ruled out across far north and northwest \nportions of the area. A very low but nonzero tornado threat \nexists across central VA, the northern neck, and Lower MD \nEastern Shore from now-midnight.\n\nThe severe/tornado threat has ended across south-central VA, \nand the Tornado Watch has been cancelled. Evening wx analysis \nshows that the remnant low of Isaias is tracking across south-\ncentral VA and is beginning to occlude. Winds have become SW \nacross Mecklenburg/Lunenburg Counties on the back side of the \nlow behind a weak front. A CAD airmass has remained in place \nacross most of the Piedmont into Caroline/Western Hanover \nCounties. A broken line of showers has developed near the triple\npoint and extends ESE to the Tri-Cities area. Even though low- \nlevel shear is fairly high with surface winds out of the SE \nahead of the front and near the triple point, the showers have \nstruggled to show much in the way of persistent rotation.\nTherefore, the Tornado Watch has been cancelled. Still, will \nhave to closely monitor areas near that triple point (RIC Metro-\nNorthern Neck/Eastern Shore) as the low tracks NE during the \nnext few hours. Can't completely rule out a brief tornado here \nbut confidence is too low to issue another watch.\n\nOutside of the tornado threat, these storms will likely produce\nvery heavy rainfall rates. Therefore, locally heavy rain and\nflash flooding is possible, especially if convection can\nsomewhat stall along the triple point. Convection tapers off\nthis evening from SW to NE behind the line of storms. However, \nCAMs show additional showers on the back side of the low \ntonight into early Mon morning across mainly the northern half \nof the area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief warmup is expected early next week with \ncooler temperatures later in the week. Much cooler temperatures\narrive next weekend.\n\nAn upper level ridge centered across the central CONUS \ngradually builds into the region through the week. Meanwhile, \nsurface high pressure remains centered across the Deep South \nthrough midweek before a second area of high pressure builds in \nfrom the north late in the week. This will bring a warmup with \nhighs in the 70s for most Mon, lower 80s for most Tue (upper \n70s across the Eastern Shore), and mid-upper 80s (most in the\nupper 80s) Wed. Some locations could approach 90F on Wed across\nportions of central/SE VA and NE NC. Slightly cooler\ntemperatures return from Thu to Fri behind a cold front with \nhighs in the low- mid 70s north to lower 80s south. However,\nthese temperatures are still above normal for most (apart from\nnorthern portions of the FA). Actually cooler weather returns\nnext weekend as a strong cold front moves into the region with a\nstrong area of high pressure building in behind it. Breezy NW\nwinds and highs in the mid-upper 50s NW to mid 60s SE are\nexpected next weekend underneath a strong trough. If this trough\nis fully realized, we could be looking at the first frost of the\nseason across portions of the area. The latest NBM has lows in\nthe mid-upper 30s across the Piedmont and mid-upper 40s closer\nto the coast Sat and Sun nights. Otherwise, dry weather\ncontinues for most of the week apart from the chance for some\nscattered showers Thu night into Fri across mainly N/NE \nportions of the FA.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coastal Flood Advisories and Statements continue through \nlate afternoon/early evening for portions of east-central VA \nalong the Bay, tidal Rappahannock/Potomac, and across the bayside\nof the lower Maryland and VA eastern shore for nuisance to Minor\nflooding.\n\nOngoing nuisance to minor tidal flooding this afternoon/early\nevening for the middle Peninsula/northern Neck, and the lower \nMaryland eastern shore Bay zones. High tides have passed closer\nto the Ocean and across SE VA so the Statements there have ended.\nWinds becoming S-SW by tonight so additional flooding is not \nanticipated.",
